steps.body:
The validation lesson already called the booking rules from a `BookingService`, and this lesson explains why they live there. To see the reason, here is `POST /v1/bookings` written the quick way, as a `bookings/BookingController.java` that does everything itself:

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/v1/bookings")
class BookingController {

    private final List<Booking> bookings = new ArrayList<>();
    private long nextId = 1001;

    @PostMapping
    ResponseEntity<?> create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateBookingRequest in) {
        Room room = ROOMS.stream().filter(r -> r.id() == in.roomId()).findFirst().orElse(null);
        if (room == null) {
            return ResponseEntity.unprocessableContent().body(Map.of("error", "no room has this id"));
        }
        long minutes = Duration.between(in.startsAt(), in.endsAt()).toMinutes();
        if (minutes < 30 || minutes > 180) {
            return ResponseEntity.unprocessableContent().body(Map.of("error", "a booking lasts 30 minutes to 3 hours"));
        }
        if (!in.startsAt().isAfter(Instant.now())) {
            return ResponseEntity.unprocessableContent().body(Map.of("error", "must start in the future"));
        }
        if (in.attendees() > room.capacity()) {
            return ResponseEntity.unprocessableContent().body(Map.of("error", room.name() + " is too small"));
        }
        boolean clash = bookings.stream().anyMatch(b -> b.roomId() == in.roomId()
                && b.startsAt().isBefore(in.endsAt()) && in.startsAt().isBefore(b.endsAt()));
        if (clash) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT).body(Map.of("error", "room already booked"));
        }
        Booking booking = new Booking(nextId++, in.roomId(), 1, in.startsAt(), in.endsAt(),
                in.attendees(), in.note(), BookingStatus.CONFIRMED, 1, Instant.now());
        bookings.add(booking);
        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/v1/bookings/" + booking.id())).body(booking);
    }
}
```

It works, and for a weekend project it's fine. But look at how many kinds of work are mixed together in one function: reading HTTP, looking up the room, the booking rules, reading the clock, storing the booking, and shaping the JSON. It even returns the stored record as the response, so any field added to `Booking` later, an internal one included, goes to every client. And a controller is a single object shared by every request, so two bookings at once both change `bookings` and `nextId` at the same moment, which an `ArrayList` isn't built for. Each kind of work changes for a different reason, and in one function, each change risks the others:

- **The database arrives in the lesson on raw SQL.** Every line that touches `bookings`, the in-memory list, has to change, and those lines are scattered among the rules.
- **A rule changes.** The library extends bookings to 21 days ahead. You have to find the rule inside HTTP code, and the only way to test it is to send real requests.
- **A second way in.** A nightly job wants to cancel the bookings for a room that's closing, with the same rules. It isn't an HTTP request, so it can't call a handler.
- **Tests.** Checking that a three-and-a-half-hour booking is refused means starting a server, sending JSON, and reading JSON back, for what is really one comparison. And with the real clock inside the handler, a test that passes today fails next week, when its date is in the past.

## Separation of concerns

The fix is an old idea called **separation of concerns**: give each kind of work its own place, so each piece has one reason to change. A **concern** is one kind of work, like HTTP, the business rules, or storage. When the concerns are apart:

- Changing how data is stored touches only the storage code.
- A rule can be tested by calling one function with plain values and a fixed clock.
- The nightly job calls the same rule code the handler does.
- A newcomer knows where to look: HTTP problems in one place, rule bugs in another, slow queries in a third.

The cost is more files, and a little code to connect them. For an API that will live for years and be changed by many people, that cost pays for itself the first week. The rest of this lesson splits Roombook along exactly these lines, and every later lesson builds on the split.

Splitting a program by concern has a classic shape, older than the web, called **MVC**: Model, View, Controller. It was designed in the 1970s for desktop programs with windows and buttons. The **model** held the data and its rules, the **view** drew it on the screen, and the **controller** took the user's clicks and keys and told the model what to do. Web frameworks borrowed it for sites that build HTML pages on the server: the controller handles the request, the model loads the data, and the view is a template that fills the data into a page.

An API has no screen and no templates, but the same three jobs are still there, redrawn for a request and a response:

```diagram layers-and-mvc-mvc
MVC for a JSON API: the controller speaks HTTP, the model holds the domain, and the view is the JSON shape.
```

In step 1, a request arrives at the controller. In step 2, the controller calls the model to do the work. In step 3, it hands the result to the view, and in step 4, the view's JSON goes back as the response.

- The **model** is your domain: types like `Booking` and `Room`, and the rules about them. The **domain** is the real-world subject the software is about, here rooms, bookings, and the people who book them. The model knows nothing about HTTP or JSON.
- The **view** is the JSON you send back. It's a deliberate shape, not a dump of the model: camelCase field names, times as RFC 3339 text in UTC, and only what the client should see. In code, it's a small type or function that turns a model into that shape.
- The **controller** is the handler. It speaks HTTP: it reads the path, query, and body, parses them with the schemas from the validation lesson, calls the model, and turns the result into a status code, headers, and a view.

## Thin controllers

The rule that keeps MVC useful is that **controllers stay thin**. A controller translates between HTTP and the domain, and does nothing else. It never decides whether a booking is allowed, and never touches storage. A good test: if you can describe a line of a controller without the words request, response, status, header, or JSON, that line probably belongs somewhere else.

Here are Roombook's three pieces for bookings. The model, in `bookings/Booking.java`, is a record with no Spring in it:

```java
public record Booking(
        long id,
        long roomId,
        long userId,
        Instant startsAt,
        Instant endsAt,
        int attendees,
        String note,
        BookingStatus status,
        int version,
        Instant createdAt) {
}
```

The view is `bookings/BookingView.java`, the record from the request data lesson, with a method that builds it from the model. It looks almost the same as the model today, and that's fine: the point is that it can change separately. Jackson writes an `Instant` as RFC 3339 text in UTC by default:

```java
public record BookingView(
        long id,
        long roomId,
        long userId,
        Instant startsAt,
        Instant endsAt,
        int attendees,
        String note,
        BookingStatus status,
        int version,
        Instant createdAt) {

    public static BookingView from(Booking b) {
        return new BookingView(b.id(), b.roomId(), b.userId(), b.startsAt(), b.endsAt(),
                b.attendees(), b.note(), b.status(), b.version(), b.createdAt());
    }
}
```

And the controller, in `bookings/BookingController.java`, where every line is about HTTP:

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/v1/bookings")
class BookingController {

    private static final long ADA = 1;
    private final BookingService service;

    BookingController(BookingService service) {
        this.service = service;
    }

    @PostMapping
    ResponseEntity<BookingView> create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateBookingRequest body) {
        Booking booking = service.create(new NewBooking(body.roomId(), ADA, body.startsAt(),
                body.endsAt(), body.attendees(), body.note()));
        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/v1/bookings/" + booking.id()))
                .body(BookingView.from(booking));
    }

    @GetMapping("/{bookingId:\\d{1,18}}")
    BookingView get(@PathVariable long bookingId) {
        return BookingView.from(service.get(bookingId));
    }
}
```

It parses with `@Valid @RequestBody`, builds the domain's `NewBooking` record from the body and the user's id, calls the service, and turns the result into a `ResponseEntity` with a status, a `Location` header, and a view. `ADA` stands in for the signed-in user until the authentication lesson adds login. The service is the part of the model that holds the rules, and the next part gives it its own layer.

## Where MVC stops

MVC says where HTTP ends and the domain begins, but its model is a big bucket: the rules, the data access, and the data types all land in it. For an API, that's too much in one place, so the next part splits the model in two.

The model splits along the line part 1 drew: rules on one side, storage on the other.

- A **service** holds the business rules for one area, like bookings. It's the code that answers "may Ada book Turing on Monday from 14:00 to 15:30?", and makes the booking if so. Once Roombook has a database, it has a second job: deciding which changes must happen together, in one database **transaction**, so they all happen or none do.
- A **repository** holds the data access for one kind of thing. It's the only code that knows where bookings live: today a map in memory, and from the lesson on raw SQL, a PostgreSQL table. That makes it the only code with SQL in it. Its methods are named in the domain's words, like `insert`, `findById`, and `countUpcoming`, not in the storage's.

With the controller from the last part, that makes three layers:

```diagram layers-and-mvc-layers
The layers call downward only. The model's types are shared by all three.
```

## Dependencies point one way

The arrows in the diagram only point down. The controller calls the service, the service calls the repository, and never the other way around. A service never reads a request or picks a status code, and a repository never applies a business rule. A layer **depends on** another when its code uses it, and this one-way rule about dependencies is what lets you replace a layer. As long as the repository keeps its methods, the service can't tell a list in memory from a database.

To make that real in code, the service doesn't depend on one particular repository. It depends on an **interface**, a description of the methods it needs, and any repository with those methods will do.

Here is the interface, in `bookings/BookingRepository.java`, and today's implementation, in `bookings/InMemoryBookingRepository.java`:

```java
public interface BookingRepository {
    Optional<Booking> findById(long id);

    Booking insert(NewBooking booking);

    int countUpcoming(long userId, Instant now);
}
```

```java
@Repository
class InMemoryBookingRepository implements BookingRepository {

    private final Map<Long, Booking> rows = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final AtomicLong nextId = new AtomicLong(1001);
    private final Clock clock;

    InMemoryBookingRepository(Clock clock) {
        this.clock = clock;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Booking> findById(long id) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(rows.get(id));
    }

    @Override
    public synchronized Booking insert(NewBooking b) {
        boolean clash = rows.values().stream().anyMatch(o ->
                o.status() == BookingStatus.CONFIRMED && o.roomId() == b.roomId()
                        && o.startsAt().isBefore(b.endsAt()) && b.startsAt().isBefore(o.endsAt()));
        if (clash) {
            throw new ConflictException("booking-overlap", "The room is already booked then.", null);
        }
        Booking row = new Booking(nextId.getAndIncrement(), b.roomId(), b.userId(), b.startsAt(),
                b.endsAt(), b.attendees(), b.note(), BookingStatus.CONFIRMED, 1, clock.instant());
        rows.put(row.id(), row);
        return row;
    }

    @Override
    public int countUpcoming(long userId, Instant now) {
        return (int) rows.values().stream()
                .filter(b -> b.userId() == userId && b.status() == BookingStatus.CONFIRMED
                        && b.startsAt().isAfter(now))
                .count();
    }
}
```

`@Repository` marks the class as a repository bean for Spring, which part 5 explains. The raw SQL lesson's `JdbcBookingRepository` implements the same interface with SQL inside, and adds `listForUser` and `cancel` to it, for listing a member's bookings and cancelling one. Until then, the `cancel` and `update` that the routes and request data lessons called stay on the in-memory class, left out here to keep it short. `RoomRepository` is the same move for rooms: an interface, which the routes lesson's `InMemoryRoomRepository` now implements. Many requests run at once, each on its own thread, and they share this one object, so it uses a `ConcurrentHashMap`, an `AtomicLong` for ids, and a `synchronized` insert, so two bookings can't both pass the overlap check at the same moment. The overlap check stands in for the database constraint the data integrity lesson builds, and the `Clock` stamps `createdAt`, the way a database fills in `now()`. The service takes everything it needs in its constructor, in `bookings/BookingService.java`:

```java sketch
@Service
public class BookingService {

    private final BookingRepository bookings;
    private final RoomRepository rooms;
    private final Clock clock;

    public BookingService(BookingRepository bookings, RoomRepository rooms, Clock clock) {
        this.bookings = bookings;
        this.rooms = rooms;
        this.clock = clock;
    }

    ...
}
```

The `Clock` is in the list too. Reading the time is a dependency like any other, so a test can pass a fixed clock, and Roombook passes the course's fixed time.

## DTOs at the edges

The types that cross the HTTP boundary are called **DTOs**, short for data transfer objects: the parsed request body coming in, from the validation lesson, and the view going out. They're kept apart from the model on purpose. The create DTO has no `id`, `status`, or `createdAt`, because the client mustn't set them. The view has no internal fields, because the client mustn't see them. And the model can gain a field, like a flag for a booking that staff created, without it leaking into a single response or becoming something a client can send.
